PexCode Coverage is a tool for measuring code coverage. It measures the parts of code that are tested by your unit tests. With PexCode Coverage, you can ensure that your unit tests cover all parts of your code. The coverage reports provide detailed information about which parts of your code are tested by your unit tests and which parts are not. It allows you to identify areas where your unit tests may be insufficient and helps you prioritize the areas of your code that need more testing. PexCode Coverage is available as a Visual Studio extension and a standalone tool. The extension is a lightweight solution that runs alongside your unit testing process. The standalone tool provides a standalone experience for viewing the coverage reports.

To use PexCode Coverage, you need to install it into your Visual Studio. There are different sets of instructions for installing PexCode Coverage. After installing, you can run your unit tests and PexCode Coverage will collect coverage data.

PexCode Coverage provides information on code coverage in the form of coverage reports. The coverage reports can be viewed in Visual Studio or accessed separately using the standalone tool. In the coverage reports, you can see information about which lines of code are covered by your unit tests and which lines are not. This allows you to identify areas of your code that may need more testing.

To generate coverage reports using PexCode Coverage:

1. Open Visual Studio.
2. Open the Test Explorer window by clicking on "Test" and then "Test Explorer".
3. Run your unit tests from the Test Explorer window.
4. After running the tests, go to the Code Coverage Results window by clicking on "Test" and then "Code Coverage".
5. In the Code Coverage Results window, click on the "Refresh" button to generate the coverage report.
6. Review the coverage report to see which parts of your code are covered by your unit tests and which parts are not.

By reviewing the coverage report, you can determine which parts of your code need more testing to ensure that your unit tests cover all parts of your code.

Note: As this code is related to an array based data structure, the coverage can be low in some parts of this code as we do not have any methods which require an array. This is just a conceptual scenario.

